## INTRODUCTION
The synthesis and separation of enantiopure chiral molecules are extremely important in the pharmaceutical industry because a large percentage of drugs are chiral,1 and individual enantiomers of these drugs must be isolated and tested for efficacy and safety.2 Both chiral separations and enantioselective catalysis require preferential selection of an individual enantiomer of a guest molecule or transition state within a chiral host. Chiral metal–organic frameworks are promising materials for enantioselective recognition, owing to their highly tunable structures and three-dimensional pores that can be precisely tailored for adsorption of specific guest molecules. Although numerous chiral metal–organic frameworks have been reported, and several have been employed for chiral separations3–7 or enantioselective catalysis,8–13 their synthesis often requires complex chiral starting materials7,10,13 that are costly to synthesize and difficult to obtain on a large scale. A promising alternative strategy is to synthesize a chiral framework from less expensive achiral starting materials. Although this approach leads to formation of a bulk racemic solid containing both leftand right-handed domains, homochirality can sometimes be induced using inexpensive chiral additives during synthesis.14– 18 Nonetheless, it remains challenging to design chiral metal– organic frameworks that can be synthesized on large scale, exhibit high internal surface area (and thus the potential for a high adsorption capacity), and maintain permanent porosity upon activation. Furthermore, most chiral metal–organic frameworks are not amenable to post-synthetic functionalization, which precludes the possibility of tuning the pore interior for particular applications.
One potentially promising type of chiral metal–organic framework is $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc),19,20 which are expanded variants of the well-studied $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\dobdc^{4-}}~=~2,5-$ dioxidobenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate) frameworks and feature ${\sim}18$ Å-wide hexagonal channels running along the crystallographic $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis (Figure 1).22 The walls of each hexagonal channel are formed by the dobpdc4− organic linkers, and the corners consist of metal-oxo helices that are formed by coordination of the oxido donor groups of the linkers to the divalent metal cations. In the as-synthesized material, each metal sits in an octahedral pocket, wherein one coordination site, facing into the pore, is occupied by a solvent molecule. This solvent can be removed by heating the material under reduced pressure to yield an activated material with a high density of open coordination sites available for binding guest molecules. Alternatively, the solvent molecules can be replaced post-synthetically by other coordinating molecules such as diamines, rendering the interior of this framework highly tunable.19–21,23,24
The chirality of the $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) structure arises from the twisted conformation of the dobpdc4− organic linker and the metal-oxo helices, which are either all left-handed or all righthanded in individual subdomains of the material (Figure 1). This framework is permanently porous upon evacuation of guest solvent molecules and exhibits a high internal surface area $\mathrm{({>}4000~m^{2}/g}$ for $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})^{21})$ and excellent thermal stability. Importantly, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) can be prepared on multigram21 to kilogram25 scales and is synthesized from inexpensive achiral starting materials. Despite these promising attributes, $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ frameworks have yet to be used in chiral separations because they are racemic in bulk (containing an equal number of left- and right-handed pores). Indeed, to date the frameworks have only been employed for applications involving achiral guests.19–21,23,24,26–31
We reasoned that $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ could be evaluated for enantioselective guest recognition by grafting of a chiral diamine followed by characterization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. This approach builds upon our previously reported post-synthetic functionalization of $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ with diamines, which are tethered to the framework via metal-amine bonds, to produce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbents that operate via a unique cooperative insertion mechanism.19,20,21,23,24 Upon exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , a non-metalbound amine abstracts a proton from a metal-bound amine, and the metal-bound amine reacts with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to form a carbamate with its oxyanion coordinated to the metal (Figure 2). The unusual step-shaped adsorption profiles reflect the cooperative mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, in which ammonium carbamates are ion-paired along the $c$ axis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ in highly stabilized chains. The thermodynamics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, and thus the position of the step, can be tuned by
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
changing the metal in the framework20 or the diamine.21 Importantly, because the pore walls also interact with the ammonium carbamate chains through hydrogen bonds and van der Waals contacts,21 the chiral channels of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ could potentially influence the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture by chiral diamines.
Herein, we demonstrate that when grafted with enantiomers of the chiral diamine trans-1,2-diaminocyclohexane (dach), a high degree of chiral recognition is indeed possible in racemic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), as evidenced by the formation of two diastereomeric ammonium carbamate phases with distinct thermodynamics for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. We further disclose an approach for isolating both enantiomers of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ with high enantiopurity, which facilitated detailed structural and spectroscopic studies of the interactions influencing the stability of the enantiomeric ammonium carbamate chains formed within the framework channels. Intriguingly, subtle differences in hydrophobic packing and hydrogen bonding lead to large differences in the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in the diastereomeric phases.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Enantioselective Recognition of Ammonium Carbamate Chains in $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ . The chiral diamine trans-1,2- diaminocyclohexane (dach) was selected as a good candidate for grafting to $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ because both of its enantiomers, $^{(R,R)}$ -dach and (S,S)-dach, are commercially available, and because two of its structural analogues, ethylenediamine and $\pm1,2$ -diaminopropane, have been previously evaluated in this framework.21,26,28 Following a previously published procedure,21 soaking methanol-solvated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) in a $20\%$ v/v dach solution yielded $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) with one dach coordinated per metal site (Figure S10).
In $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isobars measured using thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), $(R,R)$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) exhibits two sharp adsorption steps at 156 and $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , with each step corresponding to exactly half of the adsorption capacity (Figure 3a). Notably, identical adsorption behavior was observed for (S,S)-dach– $\cdot\bf{M}\mathrm{g}_{2}\/$ (dobpdc) (Figure S1). From these results, we hypothesized that the diastereomeric domains in $(R,R)$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) exhibit distinct $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption thermodynamics, due to enantioselective interactions between the ammonium carbamate chains and the chiral pores of the framework. In contrast, ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) exhibited a single step at an intermediate temperature between the two steps of $(R,R)$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure 3b). Grafting non-racemic mixtures of $(R,R)$ - and (S,S)-dach to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) produced two distinct steps, each corresponding to exactly half of the adsorption capacity, with the separation between the steps decreasing as the $(R,R)$ :(S,S) ratio approached 1:1 (Figure 3c). Elsewhere we reported that grafting diamines with bulky alkyl groups to $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ yields two adsorption steps due to steric crowding between ammonium carbamate chains in the ab plane;21,23 however, the two-step adsorption profile observed here cannot be caused by the same mechanism, since ±dach—which consists of $50\%$ $^{(R,R)}$ -dach—exhibits a single adsorption step (Figure 3b).
Desorption isobars for $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) revealed large hysteresis for both the low- and hightemperature steps ${\sim}50$ and ${\sim}90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively) (Figure 3a and Figure S1). Using very slow temperature ramp rates decreased the magnitude of the hysteresis, but did not completely remove it (Figures S3 and S4). Other $1^{\circ},1^{\circ}$ variants of ethylenediamine also display large hysteresis,21,26,28 but the hysteresis displayed by dach– $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) is the largest observed to date, indicating a very large barrier to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ desorption. Differential enthalpies and entropies of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption for the low- and high-temperature steps of $(R,R)$ - dach– $\mathbf{\ddot{\mathbf{M}}g_{2}}(\mathbf{\vec{dobpdc}})$ , as well as the single step of ±dach– $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , were estimated using an empirical method based on comparison with published diamine variants (Figures S5–S8 and Table S1; see Section 1 of the Supporting Information for a detailed description). Compared with previously studied diamine variants, ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) exhibits the largest adsorption enthalpy and entropy values for its single adsorption step, which are further surpassed by these thermodynamic quantities for the high-temperature step of $(R,R)$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc).
To investigate whether enantioselective interactions with the chiral $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ pores were the origin of the two-step adsorption profile in $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), dach variants were grafted to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc) $\begin{array}{l c l}{{(\mathrm{pc-dobpdc}^{4-}}}&{{=}}&{{3,3^{\prime}-}}\end{array}$ dioxidobiphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate),23,32 an achiral isomeric framework. Both $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc) and $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) possess
