hexagon-shaped channels with diameters of ${\sim}18\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and contain a 1:1 ratio of left- and right-handed helices (Figures S15 and S16). However, whereas subdomains of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ contain helices of a single handedness, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc) crystallizes in the achiral space group $R\bar{3}$ with an equal number of alternating left- and right-handed helices within each pore. Furthermore, the two phenyl rings of pc-dobpdc4− are coplanar in the framework, corresponding to an achiral conformation.23
Isobars for the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pcdobpdc) and ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc) both exhibit only a single adsorption step (Figures S17–S19). Therefore, the two adsorption steps in $(R,R)$ -dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) cannot be accounted for simply by the presence of left- and right-handed metal-oxo helices. Instead, these results suggest that entire chiral channels of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ influence the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.
To further understand the interactions responsible for this observed enantioselectivity, we sought to study the structure of the ±dach-appended frameworks, both before and after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Because we were unable to grow $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) single crystals of sufficient size for $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction, we utilized $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ for these experiments. Alkylethylenediamine-appended $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ frameworks are isostructural to their $\mathbf{Mg}$ counterparts and follow the same $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption mechanism.21 As expected, like its $\mathbf{Mg}$ congener, $(R,R)$ -dach- $.Z\mathrm{n}_{2}$ (dobpdc) exhibited two $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ desorption steps and large hysteresis upon desorption (Figure S23). Since all single crystals of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ were found to be racemically twinned, containing left- and right-handed subdomains, grafting enantiopure dach would create diastereomeric subdomains with a potentially unsolvable diffraction pattern. In contrast, the enantiomeric subdomains of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ remained enantiomeric after grafting of ±dach, leading to a solvable diffraction pattern.
The crystal structure of ±dach– $\cdot Z\mathrm{n}_{2}$ (dobpdc) displayed wellresolved density for the base framework, with unit cell parameters $(a=b=21.4931$ (13) $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , $c=6.6895(5)\ \mathring\mathrm{A};$ ) similar to those of the framework solvated by dimethylacetamide $\overset{\cdot}{\boldsymbol{a}}=$ $b=21.526(4)\stackrel{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ , $c=6.7042$ (15) Å).21 However, the diamines were highly disordered in the structure, even at $100~\mathrm{K}$ , and no clear conformers could be resolved.
In contrast, upon $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dosing, well-defined conformers were resolved for the ammonium carbamate chains, with $(R,R).$ - and (S,S)-dach present in approximately a 1:1 ratio (Figure 4). In the crystal structure, each ammonium carbamate unit is hydrogen-bonded to its neighbor along the $c$ axis to form highly stabilized chains (Figure 4c), consistent with other diamine-appended variants of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ . The N–H group of each carbamate was also observed to hydrogen bond to carboxylate O atoms in the metal-oxo helix, providing additional stabilization to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -adsorbed phase. Additional hydrogen bonds were also resolved between neighboring ammonium carbamate chains in the ab plane (Figure 4d), a feature that has not been observed in any other ethylenediamine-appended $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ crystal structures to date. This latter interaction was enabled by a unit cell contraction of $4.1\%$ in the $a b$ plane $(a=b=20.6012(7)\ \mathring{\mathring{\$ ) relative to the starting $\pm\mathrm{dach-Zn_{2}(d o b p d c)}$ framework, which brings pairs of ammonium carbamate chains into close proximity, accompanied by a slight expansion along the $c$ axis $\overset{\cdot}{c}=6.8\overset{\cdot}{9}39(3)\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}\overset{\cdot}{,}$ . We note that the hydrogen bonds in the ab plane must be sufficiently strong to induce this otherwise
unfavorable distortion, and they likely contribute to the thermodynamic favorability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in the material.
Importantly, Rietveld refinement of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -inserted structure of ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) against the corresponding powder X-ray diffraction pattern confirmed that it is isostructural to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted ±dach– $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure S24). The contraction in the $a b$ plane observed for the $Z\mathrm{n}$ structure upon $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dosing is also consistent with powder $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Sigma}}_{\mathrm{X}}$ -ray diffraction data obtained for $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and several other $1^{\circ},1^{\circ}$ -diamine-grafted $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ variants (Figure S13).26,28 We note that the large hysteresis observed for ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and other $1^{\circ}-1^{\circ}$ -diamine-appended variants21 may arise from the substantial unit cell contraction and/or formation of stabilizing hydrogen bonds in the $a b$ plane upon $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, leading to a large activation barrier for the collective phase transition.33
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted crystal structure offers insight into why grafting varying mixtures of $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach yielded two distinct $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption steps with tunable positions (Figure 3c). In this structure, the ammonium nitrogen, two carbons from the cyclohexane ring, and all carbamate atoms of $(R,R)$ - dach can be superimposed with the corresponding atoms of (S,S)-dach (Figure 4b). The structure suggests that the $(R,R)$ - and (S,S)-dach enantiomers are distributed homogeneously throughout the framework and capture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cooperatively to form mixed-stereochemistry ammonium carbamate chains with intermediate stability. Consistent with this analysis, it was found that various combinations of $1^{\circ}-1^{\circ}.$ - ethylenediamine analogues yielded a perfectly tunable step of intermediate temperature (Figures S20–S22 and Table S2). These results suggest that when enriched with $^{(R,R)}$ -dach, the corresponding ammonium carbamate chains interact more favorably with one enantiomer of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , while (S,S)- dach-enriched chains interact more favorably with the opposite $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ enantiomer. The enantiomers of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) interact equivalently with racemic ammonium carbamate chains, accounting for the single $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption step exhibited by ±dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc).
We note that because the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture can be tuned by varying the ratio of $(R,R)$ :(S,S) dach in the framework, the conformations of the $(R,R)$ and (S,S) ammonium carbamates in ±dach– $.\mathbf{M}_{2},$ (dobpdc) are not necessarily representative of the structure of enantiopure dach– ${\bf{\cdot}}{\bf{M}}_{2}$ (dobpdc). In order to further understand the structural basis for the enantioselective ammonium carbamate recognition by $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , we deemed it necessary to study the structure of enantiopure ammonium carbamate chains within enantiopure $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ . Because the racemic nature of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc)—both the $Z\mathrm{n}$ single crystals and $\mathbf{Mg}$ powder— prevented this structure from being obtained, we sought to prepare enantiopure $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc).
Synthesizing Enantiopure $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ by Chiral Induction. In attempting to prepare enantiopure $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), we first sought to establish an assay to characterize the enantiopurity. We considered techniques such as single-crystal X-ray diffraction and solid-state circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy. The former technique does not indicate bulk chirality, however, and the small size of the of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}^{}$ (dobpdc) single crystals exclude single-crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray diffraction as a viable method.21 While enantiomeric materials exhibit opposite signals via solid-state CD spectroscopy,34 this method cannot quantify enantiopurity unless an enantiopure standard is available.14 Instead, we devised the following simple assay to characterize $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) enantiopurity: grafting with enantiopure dach followed by collection of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isobars. Indeed, whereas $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ exhibited two steps with exactly equal heights, enantio-enriched $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) samples are expected to deviate from this 1:1 ratio. This assay therefore enables quantification   of both $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) enantiopurity and phase purity, since the height and sharpness of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption steps are dependent on the purity and porosity of the material. The quantitative nature of this assay could be further validated by solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted, dachappended $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ variants, as discussed below.
A variety of commercially-available, non-toxic, and chemically diverse compounds were examined for inducing uniform chirality in $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , including amino acids, inexpensive chiral alcohols, and the previously-reported chiral inducing agents camphoric acid and cinchonine (Figure S28).15,16 Because the mechanism of nucleation for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) crystals is unknown, it was difficult to predict $a$ priori which additives would induce homochirality without disrupting framework assembly. However, the amino acid Lproline was a particularly attractive candidate because it is an enantioselective organocatalyst,35 can induce typically achiral MOF-5 to distort into a semi-stable chiral structure,36 and can facilitate the growth of large zirconium-based metal–organic framework crystals.37
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ was initially carried out in the presence of each chiral reagent with an additive:H4dobpdc molar ratio of 3:1, previously employed successfully for chiral induction of a metal–organic framework.15 The enantiopurity and quality of each resulting $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ sample was assessed via examination of the sharpness of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption steps and quantitation of the combined step heights (Figures S29–S37). The majority of the additives caused detectable chiral induction of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , and for samples displaying chiral induction grafting $(R,R)$ - or (S,S)-dach yielded exactly opposite high- and low-temperature $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption step behavior. Namely, high- and low-temperature steps dominated for $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach-appended frameworks, respectively, confirming enrichment of one $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) enantiomer (Figure S32). Taking into account both the extent of chiral induction and framework quality, we found the optimal additives to be L-proline and D-panthenol (an inexpensive, industrially-produced provitamin derivative of vitamin ${\bf B}_{5}$ and an ingredient in moisturizers and lotions38).
Hence, we focused our remaining investigations on frameworks prepared in the presence of these compounds. For example, by carrying out the synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ in the presence of 5 molar equivalents of L-proline (relative to H4dobpdc), it was possible to optimize the framework enantiomeric purity to $90\%$ (Figure S37). As expected, $L-$ and D-proline yielded opposite enantiomers of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , and synthesis in the presence of ±proline yielded $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ (Figure S37). Adding 10 or more equivalents of proline in the reaction dramatically decreased the $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ yield, while in the presence of 20 of equivalents proline, no $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) was formed even after 30 days.
$^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) further displayed sharp $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption steps and high overall $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, equal to that of $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , indicating excellent sample quality.
Unexpectedly, the opposite enantiomer of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) could be obtained using the same solvent system and $\mathbf{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}}{:}\mathbf{H_{4}d_{4}}$ obpdc ratio, but with a five-fold increase in the concentrations of both $\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and H4dobpdc (Figure 5c). When grafted with $^{(R,R)}$ -dach and (S,S)-dach, this material displayed a reversal in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption step temperatures relative to the $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ analogues  (Figure 5c). This material, which we refer to as $S{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ , was thus identified as the enantiomer of $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ , and it exhibited ${\sim}92\%$ enantiomeric purity and excellent sample quality based on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of its dominant adsorption step. Qualitative characterization of the bare $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S.$ - $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ materials using solid-state CD spectroscopy (measured via transmission and diffuse reflectance) confirmed that these materials are indeed enantiomers of each other (Figure 6a and Figures S51–S53).
To determine the absolute stereochemistry of $R$ - and $S.$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), we compared their experimental solid state CD spectra with calculated spectra for left- and right-handed $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) enantiomers (i.e., materials exhibiting exclusively left- or right-handed helices, respectively) (Figure 6b). Computational models were prepared for several structural motifs of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , including one, two, or three stacked dobpdc4− molecules coordinated to two, four, or six $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ cations, respectively, as well as a segment of the metal-oxo helix consisting of seven truncated (one phenyl ring included) or seven full dobpdc4− molecules (see Figure S54). The motifs corresponding to the left-handed (right-handed) structure all exhibited an initial intense positive (negative) CD band in the UV-Vis spectral region, at wavelengths above $350\ \mathrm{nm}$ (peak positions were sensitive to the choice of the functional), except in the case of the truncated metal-oxo helix model, which exhibited weak intensity (Figure S56). For all models, aggregation of multiple dobpdc4− units led to an increase in band intensities that was proportional to the number of dobpdc4− units, although only minor qualitative changes were observed in the spectral shapes. The models therefore predict the presence of a CD band above ${\sim}350\ \mathrm{nm}$ that is caused predominantly by the twist direction of the dobpdc4− moieties, followed by CD intensity of opposite sign at shorter wavelengths (Figure 6b). This sign pattern is in agreement with the experimental solid-state CD spectra (Figure 6a), and therefore the calculations strongly support the assignment of $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ and $\mathbf{S}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ as being the right- and left-handed $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ enantiomers, respectively (i.e., exhibiting right- and left-handed helices) (Figure 6b and Figures S54–S56).
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ in the presence of ±panthenol yielded $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure S41), confirming that enantiopure panthenol is necessary for chiral induction. As expected, $R\cdot$ -, $S-$ , and $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ produced identical powder X-ray diffraction patterns (Figure S47) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isobars after grafting with ±dach (Figure S49). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) further revealed very similar particle morphologies and sizes for these materials (Figure S48). Notably, after seven months of storage in methanol, samples of both $S{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and R-Mg2(dobpdc) exhibited no loss in enantiopurity or sample quality, based on characterization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and relative step heights after grafting with enantiopure dach (Figure S50).
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ using the same D-panthenol solvent system and $\mathbf{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}}{:}\mathbf{H_{4}d o}$ bpdc ratio with a range of H4dobpdc concentrations indicated that a switch in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) handedness occurs between 107 and $140\ \mathrm{mM}$ H4dobpdc (Figure 7). For H4dobpdc concentrations ${>}140~\mathrm{mM}$ , $S{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) remained the predominant enantiomer. The mechanism leading to the switch in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) handedness is unclear, although we speculate that the mechanism for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) nucleation may be different depending on the reaction conditions, and D-panthenol may exert different stereochemical influences on the various nucleation species. However, because the mechanism of nucleation for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) is not known, and it is in general extremely challenging to probe nucleation in metal–organic frameworks,39 further investigation will be necessary to elucidate the mechanism of chiral induction by D-panthenol.
We attempted to obtain large, enantiopure single crystals of $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}$ (dobpdc) by growing them in the presence D-panthenol (Table S4). However, using a D-panthenol: $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ dobpdc molar ratio of 3:1, 10:1, or 20:1 yielded only twinned single crystals exhibiting very slight chiral induction (Table S4), and higher molar quantities of D-panthenol $(\geq40)$ produced only polycrystalline powder. Notably, in the crystal structure of racemic $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ synthesized with D-panthenol, DMA solvent molecules are bound to the metal sites in the framework, in spite of the presence of D-panthenol in the mother liquor. This structure is consistent with that of the crystals obtained as-synthesized without D-panthenol,21 suggesting that D-panthenol does not bind strongly to the metal sites of the already-assembled Zn2(dobpdc) framework and supporting the hypothesis that the role of D-panthenol in chiral induction may be related to $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) nucleation, rather than growth.
The Structural Basis for Enantioselective Recognition of Ammonium Carbamate Chains. With a synthetic route in hand to access $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) frameworks with high enantiopurity, we could further investigate the structural basis for enantioselective recognition of chiral ammonium carbamate chains. Synchrotron powder $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray diffraction
