Density functional theory (DFT) calculations provided further evidence for the absolute stereochemistry of $R$ - Mg2(dobpdc) and $S{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ . Models for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach bound to $R{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (i.e. the enantiomer of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ containing right-handed helices) were prepared assuming the same unit cell and structure as ±dach– $-Z{\bmod{2}}$ (dobpdc). When the unit cell parameters were then allowed to vary freely during DFT structure optimization, the optimized $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\bf-M}\mathrm{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) structure was contracted in the $a b$ plane and expanded slightly along $c$ , relative to the (S,S)-dach structure (Table S8). These trends in the DFT results qualitatively match the experimental powder X-ray diffraction data and further confirm the stereochemical assignment obtained from solid-state CD spectra (Figure 6). Moreover, the calculated enthalpies for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach– $R$ -Mg2(dobpdc) indicated that the $(R,R)$ structure is more stable by approximately $80\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Figure S64), consistent with the more thermodynamically favorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf-}R{\bf-}{\bf M}{\bf g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) compared to (S,S)-dach– $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ (Figure S7). Additional DFT calculations carried out assuming different unit cell parameters supported the conclusion that $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ is the righthanded enantiomer of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ (Figure S64). Interestingly, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\bf-M}{\bf g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) was found to be more stable than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted (S,S)-dach– $\scriptstyle-R$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) in all of the DFT calculations, regardless of what unit cell parameters were assumed, indicating that greater stability in the DFT calculations was not caused simply by contraction in the ab plane.
We employed solid-state NMR spectroscopy to further characterize the diastereomeric ammonium carbamate phases and to confirm the absolute stereochemistry of $R$ - and $S.$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc). DFT calculations for $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach– $R$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) showed distinct chemical shifts for the carbamate carbon, the carbamate $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ hydrogen, and the ammonium hydrogen atoms (Figures 8a, S65, and S66 and Table S7), as anticipated for diastereomeric structures.40 The ability of DFT to accurately optimize candidate structures of crystalline materials and predict NMR parameters is now well established, and comparison of experimental and calculated parameters (in particular $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ chemical shifts) for different trial structures can facilitate structure determination.41–46 Experimental magic-angle spinning solid state NMR spectra were collected for dach– $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ variants following dosing with isotopically enriched $(99\ \%\ ^{13}\mathrm{C})$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .24 Cross polarization from $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ to $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ enabled selective detection of chemisorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and eliminated background signal from gasphase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Carbon resonances from the H4dobpdc linker and the diamine alkyl backbone are visible in these spectra, although the intensities are much weaker because $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ is present in natural abundance $(1\%)$ at these positions.
Carbon dioxide chemisorbed in the $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $R$ - $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ and $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot S\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) frameworks exhibits distinct $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ chemical shifts, consistent with the DFT calculations (Figure 8b). The spectrum of the $^{(R,R)}$ -dach–S$\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ framework displays a major chemisorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ resonance at $163.4~\mathrm{ppm}$ , a value that is similar to resonances observed for other ammonium carbamate chains formed in this material.24 In contrast, the $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) framework exhibits a major $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ resonance at $161.6\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , which is closer to chemical shifts previously observed for carbamic acids in metal-organic frameworks24,47 and may be indicative of stronger carbamate ion-pairing in this diastereomeric phase.
Importantly, the qualitative agreement between the theoretical and measured $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ chemical shifts for chemisorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ further supports our earlier assignment of $R{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and $S{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ as the right- and left-handed structures, respectively. In contrast to the enantiopure dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) structures, $^{13}\mathrm{C}.$ -solid state NMR spectroscopic characterization of ±dach– $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) revealed a single broad feature at ${\sim}162\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ , suggestive of a distribution of different local chemical environments for the carbamate that arise due to the random, statistical distribution of $^{(R,R)}$ - and (S,S)-dach within the crystallites (Figure S68).
The $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra also enabled quantitation of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ enantiopurity, corroborating the values determined from the TGA step height assay (Figure 8b). Quantitation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted $(R,R)$ -dach– $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) by solid-state NMR spectroscopy indicates a 1:1 ratio (within error) of $R\cdot$ - and $S{\mathrm{-Mg}}_{2}$ (dobpdc), confirming the validity of the NMR quantitation approach (see Methods for further details). Similarly, the enantiopurities extracted from these NMR experiments agree well with values determined using TGA step height quantification for $95\%$ , $19\%$ , and $12\%$ $R\mathrm{.}$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure 8b).
Further insight into the diastereomeric structures was gained using 2D HETCOR solid-state NMR spectroscopy. In this experiment, cross polarization from $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ to $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ enables detection of chemisorbed $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ , while also revealing the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ chemical shifts of the hydrogen atoms most proximal to the carbamate carbon. For both $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\bf-M}\mathrm{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) and $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $S\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) phases, the strongest correlations with the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ carbamate carbon arise from the carbamate $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ hydrogen, an unsurprising result because this hydrogen is the closest through space. The chemical shifts of the $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ hydrogen atoms are 3.9 and $4.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively, for $^{(R,R)}$ - dach– $R$ -Mg2(dobpdc) and $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot S\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure 8c), consistent with the DFT calculated chemical shifts within $0.3\mathrm{ppm}$ and further supporting our assignments of $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S.$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (Figure 8a).
In HETCOR experiments carried out with longer contact times, correlations between the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ carbamate carbon and the ammonium hydrogen atoms were also detected (Figure S68), and the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ chemical shifts for the ammonium hydrogen atoms were found to be 7.8 and $7.2\mathrm{ppm}$ for $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $\mathbf{\mathcal{R}}$ - $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ and $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf-}S{\bf-}\mathbf{M}\mathrm{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), respectively. Differences in chemical shifts of specific nuclei between the two diastereomers can be correlated to differences in the strengths of non-covalent interactions, with larger $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ chemicals shifts indicative of stronger hydrogen bonding.48 Our results thus indicate the presence of stronger hydrogen bonding for the ammonium hydrogen atoms of
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
$^{(R,R)}$ -dach– $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ , but weaker hydrogen bonding for the carbamate N–H hydrogens, relative to (S,S)-dach– $R$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc).
Taking into account all of the data, a detailed explanation can be proposed for the different thermodynamic stabilities of the diastereomeric phases. Figure 9 presents DFT calculated structures for $^{(R,R)}$ -dach– ${\bf\mathcal{R}}{\bf-M}\mathrm{g}_{2}\mathrm{|}$ (dobpdc) and (S,S)-dach– $\mathbf{\mathcal{R}}$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), with unit cell parameters fixed to the values determined by powder $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}$ -ray diffraction (recall that the $^{(R,R)}$ - dach structure is slightly more contracted in the ab plane and slightly expanded along the $c$ axis). Space-filling models reveal more extensive van der Waals contacts for the $(R,R)$ - dach ammonium carbamates compared to (S,S)-dach, both with respect to interactions between cyclohexane rings and between the rings and the pores walls (Figure 9a). It is likely that more favorable van der Waals contacts and thus superior packing of $(R,R)$ -dach stabilizes the resulting ammonium carbamate structure.
We propose a few roles that the chiral $R{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ framework may play in the observed distinct hydrogen bonding strengths between the diastereomeric phases. The stronger hydrogen bonding in the $(R,R)$ -dach structure could arise from superior packing of the cyclohexane rings, enabling more optimal orientation of the ammonium hydrogens and carbamate oxygen for hydrogen bonding (Figure 9b). In addition, the stereochemistry of the two-carbon diamine bridge in $^{(R,R)}$ -dach leads to a different orientation of the ammonium $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}$ bond, thereby subtly affecting the positions of the ammonium hydrogen atoms and the carbamate oxygen (Figure 9b). The framework influences ammonium carbamate stability not only by direct interactions (through hydrophobic packing and hydrogen bonding with the carbamate N–H group), but also by serving as a chiral three-dimensional scaffold that impacts the strength of interactions between the enantiopure ammonium carbamates.
Because the enantioselective interactions in dach– $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) appear to be strongly correlated with  contraction of the framework structure due to hydrogen bonding, it was unclear whether $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) would exhibit broad enantioselectivity toward guest molecules that do not induce a unit cell contraction in the $a b$ plane. Therefore, $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ was evaluated for enantioselectivity in the formation of ammonium carbamate chains from another chiral diamine, 3- aminopyrrolidine (Figures S70–S74). This enantiopure diamine results in two adsorption steps upon grafting to $\pm\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , while the racemic diamine counterpart exhibits a single step, indicating enantioselective recognition by $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ (Figure S73). Previous structural work has indicated that $1^{\circ},2^{\circ}$ diamines (such as 3-aminopyrrolidine) form ammonium carbamate chains with different structures compared to dach.21 Therefore, the enantioselective recognition of diverse diamine structures by $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ is promising in terms of its generality for recognizing different chiral guest molecules.
## CONCLUSION
The foregoing results demonstrate enantioselective recognition of chiral ammonium carbamate chains by $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{\upmu}$ (dobpdc), as revealed by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption experiments. To better understand the structural basis of enantioselective recognition, a procedure to isolate enantiopure $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) was devised. Surprisingly, a single chiral additive, Dpanthenol, could be used to prepare either $R\mathrm{.}$ - or $S.$ - $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) with high enantiopurity, depending on the reactant concentrations. The high enantiopurities and crystallinities of both $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ enabled further investigation into the chiral ammonium carbamate structures. This study revealed that subtle enantioselective interactions between the ammonium carbamates and the chiral $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) framework have a substantial impact on the affinity of chiral diamine-appended $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In addition, the chiral $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) scaffolding influences the relative orientations of the ammonium carbamate chains and the strength of their hydrogen bonding interactions, both in the ab plane and along the $c$ axis.
In conjunction with its other valuable characteristics, including low cost, scalable synthesis, excellent stability, large pore diameter (18 Å), and post-synthetic tunability, the discovery of a synthetic route for enantiopure $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ opens up an array of potential applications for this promising and versatile framework. While further work is required to establish chiral recognition of guest molecules (or transition states) in solution, the detailed structural study performed here highlights the importance of one-dimensional channels and guest–guest interactions for enantioselectivity in this material. Future efforts will be directed toward exploring the enantioselective adsorption of guests capable of binding simultaneously to multiple open metal coordination sites, and to exploit stabilizing interactions between bound guest molecules.
## EXPERIMENTALSECTION
General Procedures, Materials, and Reagents. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were collected on a Bruker AMX 300 MHz NMR spectrometer, a Bruker AVB-400 MHz NMR spectrometer, or a Bruker AMX-400 MHz NMR spectrometer, as indicated in the figure captions in the Supporting Information. Laboratory powder X-ray diffraction patterns were collected using a Bruker AXS D8 Advance diffractometer with CuKα radiation ( $\lambda\ =\ 1.5418\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ . All synthetic manipulations were carried out under air unless noted otherwise. All solvents and diamines were purchased from commercial sources and used without additional purification. All chiral additives were purchased from commercial vendors and used without purification. The ligand H4dobpdc was purchased from Hangzhou Trylead Chemical Technology Co.
Multi-Gram Synthesis of Racemic $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ . Racemic Mg2(dobpdc) was synthesized on a multi-gram scale following a published procedure with slight modifications. The ligand H4dobpdc $(9.89\ \mathrm{g},36.1\ \mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $\cdot11.5\ \mathrm{g},44.9\ \mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $200~\mathrm{\mL}$ of 55:45 methanol: $N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) using sonication. The solution was filtered to remove any undissolved particulates and added to a $350~\mathrm{mL}$ glass pressure vessel with a $3\ \mathrm{cm}$ , Teflon-coated stir bar. The reactor was sealed with a Teflon cap and heated in a silicone oil bath at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ with vigorous stirring (stirring rate of at least $750\mathrm{rpm}$ ). The white powder was isolated by filtration and soaked 3 times in $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for a minimum of $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by solvent exchange by soaking 3 times in $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol for a minimum of $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Synthesis of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc). $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc) was synthesized, washed, and characterized following a previously published procedure.23
Synthesis of Diamine-Appended Variants of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (pc-dobpdc). $\mathrm{A}{-}20\ \mathrm{\mL}$ scintillation vial was charged with toluene $(4~\mathrm{mL})$ and the diamine (1 mL). In the case of enantiopure dach, diamine $(0.5~\mathrm{\g})$ was dissolved in chloroform with $0.75\%$ ethanol $\{5\mathrm{mL}\}$ ) and extensive sonication was used to dissolve as much of the solid diamine as possible. Methanol-solvated metal–organic framework $({\sim}20~\mathrm{mg})$ was filtered and washed with toluene $(20~\mathrm{mL})$ , or chloroform with $0.75\%$ ethanol $(1-5\ \mathrm{\mL})$ in the case of enantiopure dach samples (Note: the framework should not be allowed to dry completely, as doing so for an extended period can decrease crystallinity and surface area). Note that for some $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) batches, washing with $>5~\mathrm{mL}$ of chloroform with $0.75\%$ ethanol caused diamine loss. The framework was added to the diamine solution, and the vial was swirled several times and allowed to stand at room temperature for $12{-}48\mathrm{~h~}$ (grafting time was not found
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
to substantially affect the amount of amine grafted). At this time, the mixture was filtered, and the resulting powder was thoroughly washed with toluene or chloroform with $0.75\%$ ethanol $20~\mathrm{mL}$ and allowed to dry for several minutes, yielding ${\sim}30~\mathrm{mg}$ of the diamine-appended metal–organic framework. Diamine loadings were determined by suspending ${\sim}5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the diamine-appended metal–organic framework in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMSO- $d_{6}$ , adding several drops of DCl (35 wt $\%$ in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , heating until the mixture became homogenous, and analyzing the resulting solution by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Zn}_{2}(\mathbf{dobpdc})$ Single Crystals. Racemic twinned single crystals of $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{2}({\mathrm{dobpdc}})$ were synthesized as described previously.21 Attempts to synthesize enantiopure Zn2(dobpdc) single crystals by adding D-panthenol are detailed in the Supporting Information.
Synchrotron Powder X-ray Diffraction of dach– $\mathbf{-Mg_{2}(d o b p d c)}$ Variants. Activated samples were prepared by heating freshly prepared dach– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) variants $(25~\mathrm{mg})$ under flowing argon at $155^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ . The samples were allowed to cool to room temperature and transferred to an ${\bf N}_{2}.$ –filled glovebag, then gently ground into fine powders using a mortar and pestle. The ground powders were tightly packed into $1.0~\mathrm{~mm}$ borosilicate glass capillaries, the outlets were plugged with vacuum grease, and the capillaries were removed from the glovebag and flame-sealed. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ - dosed samples were prepared by placing freshly prepared dach– Mg2(dobpdc) variants $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ onto a thermogravimetric analyzer, activating them under flowing ${\bf N}_{2}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , and then cooling them to $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before switching the gas stream to pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . After $20~\mathrm{min}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -dosed samples were removed from the furnace of the thermogravimetric analyzer, gently ground into fine powders under air, and packed into $1.0~\mathrm{\mm}$ diameter borosilicate glass capillaries and flame-sealed. Note that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ does not desorb from these materials under air at room temperature (Figures S12–14).
High-resolution powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction patterns of flame-sealed samples, as well as in situ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -dosed samples, were collected with synchrotron X-ray radiation at Beamline 17-BM-B at the Advanced Photon Source of Argonne National Laboratory. The wavelength of the $X$ -rays was $0.45241\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and the diffracted $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}$ -rays were recorded by a PerkinElmer $\mathbf{\vec{a}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\vec{S}}\mathbf{\dot{i}}$ flat panel detector. An Oxford Cryosystems cryostream was used to control the experimental temperature of the samples during data collection.
Prior to in situ dosing of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and measurement of powder X-ray diffraction patterns, ${\sim}5~\mathrm{mg}$ of sample was packed into borosilicate glass capillaries of $1~\mathrm{mm}$ diameter in air. Each capillary was attached to a custom-designed gas-dosing cell equipped with a gas valve, and was then transferred to the goniometer head. All adsorbed air was removed by evacuating in situ using a turbomolecular pump. After activation at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ variable pressures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ were dosed to the samples, and the cryostream was used to change the temperature between 500 and $100\mathrm{~K~}$ . Analysis of all diffraction data (including Pawley and Rietveld refinement of the data) is detailed in the Supporting Information.
Solid-State Circular Dichroism (CD) Spectroscopy Experiments. All solid-state CD measurements were performed on a J-1500 CD spectrometer (JASCO Corporation) and measured via transmission or diffuse reflectance. Additional experimental details are provided in the Supporting Information.
Solid-State Magic-Angle Spinning $^{13}\mathbf{C}$ NMR Experiments. A freshly prepared dach-appended $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ variant $(50-100~\mathrm{mg})$ was activated under flowing argon at $155^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The sample was allowed to cool to room temperature, then transferred to an ${\bf N}_{2}$ -filled glove bag. The samples were then packed into $3.2\mathrm{mm}$ zirconia rotors. Uncapped rotors were then evacuated for at least $10\ \mathrm{min}$ inside a home-built gas-dosing manifold before dosing with isotopically enriched $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ (99 atom $\%$ $\mathit{\Pi}_{\overline{{o}}}^{\ 13}\mathrm{C},\mathit{\Pi}<3\$ atom $\%$ $^{18}\mathrm{O}$ , Sigma Aldrich), after which the samples were allowed to equilibrate for at least $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The home-built gas-dosing manifold was recently described elsewhere.24 This manifold allows rotors to be sealed under controlled gas pressures. Gas dosing was carried out at ambient temperature $({\sim}22^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Additional experimental details and description of peak quantification are provided in the Supporting Information.
Density Functional Theory Calculations. DFT calculations for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted dach-appended $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ were performed using the CASTEP code.49 The PBE (with a generalized gradient approximation) functional was used for the exchange-correlation term,50 and ultrasoft psuedopotentials were used.51 Calculations used planewave basis functions with an energy cut-off of $60~\mathrm{Ry}$ , and a $k$ - point spacing of $0.06\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}^{-1}$ , parameters which were recently found to give converged $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ chemical shifts to within $0.1\mathrm{ppm}$ for materials closely related to those studied here.24 NMR calculations were performed using the gauge including projector augmented wave (GIPAW) approach.52 Calculated chemical shifts, $\delta_{\mathrm{{so}}}$ , were obtained using; $\delta_{\mathrm{{so}}}=-(\sigma_{\mathrm{{iso}}}-\sigma_{\mathrm{{ref}}})$ , where $\sigma_{^{\mathrm{1SO}}}$ is the calculated isotropic shielding, and $\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is a reference value. For $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR, a $\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}$ value of $30.5~\mathrm{ppm}$ was used,53,54 and for $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR a $\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}$ value of $170.6\ \mathrm{ppm}$ was used.24 Initial diastereomeric structural models were generated from the single crystal structure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -inserted ±dach–Zn2(dobpdc). For the NMR calculations presented in the text, we fixed the unit cell parameters to the values obtained from powder X-ray diffraction, given our assignments of $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Mg}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ . A geometry optimization was carried prior to the NMR calculation, in which the unit cell parameters were fixed, but the atomic positions were allowed to vary. Additional NMR calculations with different assignments of the unit cell parameters only showed small variations of chemical shift (of order $0.2\mathrm{ppm}$ ). Additional details of the unit cell parameters and geometry optimizations are given in the Supporting Information. Details regarding the time-dependent DFT calculations to simulate CD spectra55,56 are provided in the Supporting Information.
Scanning Electron Microscopy. SEM samples of Mg2(dobpdc) were prepared by dispersing fine powders into methanol and drop casting the mixture onto a silicon chip. To dissipate charge, the samples were sputter coated with approximately $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ of Au (Denton Vacuum). Crystals were imaged at $5\mathrm{keV}$ and $12\upmu\mathrm{A}$ by field emission SEM (JEOL FSM6430).
